Undifferentiated Polyarthralgia
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Symmetric or asymmetric subjective joint pain | Complete absence of clinical synovitis/effusion on exam | Normal acute phase reactants (CRP/ESR)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-041
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, widespread joint pain requiring urgent evaluation to exclude acute septic polyarthritis or a major inflammatory flare.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, immobilizing joint pain across multiple limbs. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out evolving septic arthritis, acute crystalline arthropathy, or a major autoimmune crisis, preventing rapid joint destruction prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Every single joint in my body is aching so badly I can barely move, and the pain seems to jump from my knees to my wrists.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports widespread joint pain affecting multiple joint areas, primarily the large joints (knees, shoulders) and wrists. The patient describes a symmetrical and migratory pattern of discomfort that has been worsening over several weeks, culminating in tonight's severe flare. There is morning stiffness, but it distinctly lasts less than 30 minutes. The pain prominently worsens with use or changes in the weather. The patient describes profound fatigue associated with the chronic pain. There is a complete absence of systemic manifestations like new rash or fever.
Functional Status: Highly restricted mobility due to generalized pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; struggling with basic ADLs due to pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/80 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Patient appears uncomfortable and moves very slowly, but is in no acute systemic distress. An incidental review of recent clinical notes reveals a past presentation of sudden anosmia following a prior viral prodrome with clear nasal passages (resolved post-viral olfactory dysfunction), completely unrelated to tonight's musculoskeletal complaint.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive musculoskeletal examination reveals a complete absence of obvious joint swelling, effusion, or intense localized warmth across all symptomatic joints. There is an absence of palpable synovial thickening (boggy joints). The patient maintains a normal range of motion passively, though with mild pain at the extremes. Cardiopulmonary and neurological systems are intact.
Environmental Observations: Safe, single-level living environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking paracetamol with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Polyarthralgia - Status: Acute mechanical/functional pain flare.
Differentials:

Rheumatoid Arthritis - Ruled out clinically due to the lack of objective synovitis/effusion, and morning stiffness resolving in under 30 minutes.

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out due to the multi-joint migratory nature, absence of high fever, and lack of severe, hot joint effusions.

Fibromyalgia - Cannot be definitively ruled out tonight; heavily suspected due to widespread pain without inflammatory signs, but requires formal outpatient diagnostic criteria mapping.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden inability to mobilize due to severe, widespread joint pain necessitated an immediate after-hours assessment to exclude joint-destroying pathologies like acute polyarticular gout or septic arthritis. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the risk of a severe inflammatory crisis. Rigorous physical palpation and range-of-motion testing across all major joints confirmed the absolute absence of acute synovitis, effusions, or localized heat. By defining the pain as entirely subjective and non-destructive (arthralgia rather than arthritis), the clinician safely halted an unnecessary emergency department admission, providing potent symptomatic relief and securing the patient safely at home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of scheduled oral NSAIDs. The expected investigation results include a normal erythrocyte sedimentation rate and negative rheumatoid factor, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis and ruling out classic autoimmune diseases via outpatient serology.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the generalized joint stiffness, start every morning with a five-minute hot shower while performing slow, active stretches of your major joints; applying direct heat before movement physically lowers the viscosity of the joint fluid and reduces the mechanical pain of initiating movement.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days for comprehensive rheumatological serology and a formal chronic pain management plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a sudden high fever, or if a single joint rapidly becomes swollen, bright red, and violently painful to touch.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; chronic widespread pain requires detailed autoimmune and metabolic testing over time to ensure no subtle inflammatory conditions are missed.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves chronic, fluctuating pain heavily influenced by physical deconditioning, stress, and poor sleep, requiring a multimodal management approach.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you develop a new, spreading rash, or if you completely lose the ability to put any weight on one of your legs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are not injecting your joints or starting strong immunosuppressants tonight because your joints are mechanically stable and show no signs of active, damaging inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Take your anti-inflammatory medication with a full meal, and avoid prolonged periods of sitting completely still, as absolute rest will ironically worsen this type of joint pain.

